Java 21 made several concurrency and data-model features final; other names in the same discussion were still preview or version-specific.

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Status in or around Java 21</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Virtual Threads (JEP 444)</td><td>final</td><td>Useful for blocking-style concurrency; CPU work and downstream capacity still need bounds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pattern matching for <code>switch</code> (JEP 441)</td><td>final</td><td>Guarded patterns do not cover values that fail the guard</td></tr>
<tr><td>Record patterns (JEP 440)</td><td>final</td><td>Deconstruction follows the record's declared components and their null/type behavior</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sequenced Collections (JEP 431)</td><td>final</td><td>Standardizes first/last/reversed operations; it does not change collection capacity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Generational ZGC</td><td>implementation/vendor-specific</td><td>Check the selected build, collector flags, and measured pause/CPU trade-off</td></tr>
<tr><td>Structured Concurrency</td><td>preview</td><td>Preview in Java 21 and still preview in JDK 25; isolate flags and source usage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scoped Values</td><td>preview in Java 21</td><td>Final in JDK 25; do not back-port the later status to a Java 21 target</td></tr>
<tr><td>Foreign Function &amp; Memory API</td><td>not final in Java 21</td><td>Final in JDK 22, so keep that milestone separate</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

An exhaustive Java 21 <code>switch</code> must include an unguarded fallback for a variant whose guard is false:

<pre><code><span class="k">sealed interface</span> Event <span class="k">permits</span> Refund, Charge {}
<span class="k">record</span> Refund(String id, <span class="k">long</span> amountCents) <span class="k">implements</span> Event {}
<span class="k">record</span> Charge(String id, <span class="k">long</span> amountCents) <span class="k">implements</span> Event {}
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="k">switch</span> (event) {
    <span class="k">case</span> Refund(<span class="k">var</span> id, <span class="k">var</span> amount) <span class="k">when</span> amount &gt; <span class="n">0</span> -&gt; <span class="f">refund</span>(id, amount);
    <span class="k">case</span> Refund(<span class="k">var</span> id, <span class="k">var</span> amount)                 -&gt; <span class="k">throw new</span> IllegalArgumentException(<span class="s">"amount"</span>);
    <span class="k">case</span> Charge c                                      -&gt; <span class="f">charge</span>(c);
    <span class="k">case null</span>                                          -&gt; <span class="k">throw new</span> IllegalArgumentException(<span class="s">"event"</span>);
}</code></pre>

:::deep
The migration value is larger than a feature checklist:

- Virtual threads can keep blocking JDBC/HTTP code readable, but CPU-bound work needs bounded parallelism and the database, partner quota, queue, deadline, and memory budgets remain finite.
- Monitor-related pinning changed in JDK 24; native calls and other blocking paths still require an audit on the target runtime.
- A guarded pattern covers only values satisfying its guard, so the second <code>Refund</code> arm is required.
- Scoped Values became final in JDK 25, while Structured Concurrency remains preview there. Keep those lifecycle statuses separate in build and rollout policy.

For a Spring Boot blocking service, enabling virtual threads can be a small configuration change; capacity tests decide whether it is safe.
:::
